{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And his eyes were dim from seeing.</b> Because he loved Esau, for כי ציד בפיו, and it is written כי השחד יעור. Another explanation: so that Jacob would come and take the blessings. And regarding what Rashi explained, that his eyes grew dim from the smoke of idolatry — it is somewhat difficult: why did the smoke not also dim the eyes of Rebecca? And the midrash says: a parable — of one who strikes a bone vessel against an earthen vessel: the earthen vessel breaks. But one who strikes a bone vessel against a bone vessel — neither breaks from the other. Thus Isaac was created from the earth, therefore the deeds of his daughters-in-law harmed him, for they were created from the rib. But Rebecca, who was created from the bone likewise — the deeds of her daughters-in-law did not harm her." "<b>And he called to Esau his son the elder.</b> From here [we learn] that one flatters the wicked in their time. Another thing: because the world was in his hand, he called him 'elder.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The parable's final phrase לא הזיקו לה מעשה כלותיה is textually difficult: if the parable is about Isaac's daughters-in-law (the Hittite women) and their idolatrous incense harming Isaac, the parallel מעשה כלותיה referring to Rebecca is unclear, since the same daughters-in-law would not be hers; rendered hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:09.618862875Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}